# Layout and spacing

Put the thing a household needs most at the top of the screen, then routine readings, then controls they use less often. Start with a phone layout and let Home Assistant add columns as the screen gets wider.

A predictable order helps people who check the same dashboard many times a day. They learn where the front door lock is and find it without reading every card.

## Design for the screen, not the device

Design for screen widths, not for a particular phone or tablet. The same household might open one dashboard on a phone in the garden, on a tablet on the sofa and on a wall panel in the hall.

- Start with one column. Put urgent information, such as an open door or a water leak, first.
- Let sections stack on a phone. Do not shrink a wide dashboard into small cards that are hard to read.
- Keep text in a section column. A section is never wider than 500px, so lines of text stay short enough to read.

## Screen sizes

Home Assistant uses 4 screen sizes. You choose them in a card's or section's **Visibility** settings, under **Screen**.

| Screen size | Width | Where a household sees it | What Home Assistant does |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mobile | Under 768px | A phone, in the Companion app or a browser | On a phone held upright, sections stack one under another. The sidebar is behind the menu button. |
| Tablet | 768px to 1023px | A tablet held upright, or a small laptop window | 1 or 2 sections side by side. Up to 870px wide, the sidebar is behind the menu button. |
| Desktop | 1024px to 1279px | A wall panel, or a laptop | 2 or 3 sections side by side. |
| Wide | 1280px and over | A large wall panel, a desktop browser or a television | Up to 4 sections side by side, if there is room. |

Each section needs at least 320px, so Home Assistant fits in as many as there is room for, up to the maximum you set. An open sidebar takes room from the sections.

In civica, a wall panel is a screen fixed in one place, such as a tablet on the hall wall. People read it from further away and often touch it without looking closely. Give it fewer, larger cards, and put the readings people check in passing at the top.

### Layouts at each screen size

These layouts show the same invented household dashboard at each screen size, with the urgent section first each time.

| Screen size | Section order |
| --- | --- |
| Mobile | Alerts, Heating, Lights, Energy, one under another |
| Tablet | Alerts and Heating in the first row, Lights and Energy in the second |
| Wall panel | Alerts, Heating and Lights in one row, with Energy across the full width below |

