## Grid and columns

Use the native **Sections** view for a new dashboard. It sets the column widths and gaps for you, so cards line up at every screen size.

| Part | Size | Setting |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Section column | At least 320px and at most 500px wide | Set by Home Assistant |
| Gap between sections | 32px across and 24px down. 8px across on screens up to 600px wide | Set by Home Assistant |
| Sections side by side | Up to 4 by default | **Max sections side by side** in the view settings (`max_columns`) |
| Section width | 1 column, 2, 3 or full width | **Width** in the section settings (`column_span`) |
| Grid inside a section | 12 columns, with 56px rows and 8px gaps | Set by Home Assistant |

### Card widths

A card's width is a number of the section's 12 columns. Most cards choose a sensible size themselves. Change it only when the layout needs it.

| Card width | Columns | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full | 12 | A heading, a graph, a thermostat or a map |
| Half | 6 | A Tile card, which is 6 columns by default |
| Third | 4 | 3 short readings in a row, such as room temperatures |
| Quarter | 3 | A button that has a short, clear name |

<%= colour_example('layout-section-grid', callout: false) %>

This YAML shows a Heating section for an invented household. The heading and the thermostat take the full width, and 2 Tile cards share a row.

```yaml
type: grid
cards:
  - type: heading
    heading: Heating
  - type: thermostat
    entity: climate.hallway
  - type: tile
    entity: sensor.kitchen_temperature
    name: Kitchen
    grid_options:
      columns: 6
  - type: tile
    entity: sensor.bedroom_temperature
    name: Bedroom
    grid_options:
      columns: 6
```

To make the Energy section span 2 columns on a tablet or wall panel, set its width in the section settings. On a phone it still takes the full width.

```yaml
type: grid
column_span: 2
cards:
  - type: heading
    heading: Energy today
  - type: energy-usage-graph
```

[Dashboard composition and card features](../home-assistant/dashboard-composition.html) explains the Masonry, Panel and Sidebar views and which card suits each task.

### Show a card on some screens only

Use a screen condition when a card only makes sense at some widths. For example, show a large clock on the hall wall panel but not on a phone. Do not use it to hide a control that someone needs on a phone.

```yaml
type: clock
visibility:
  - condition: screen
    media_query: "(min-width: 1024px)"
```

## Spacing scale

Use the civica spacing scale in a maintained custom card. Every step is a multiple of 5px. civica has no spacing variable, so write the value in the card's CSS.

| Unit | Value | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 5px | Between an icon and its label |
| 2 | 10px | Between a label and its hint, and between items in a row |
| 3 | 15px | Inside a compact card, and between rows |
| 4 | 20px | Inside a card |
| 5 | 25px | Between groups in a busy card |
| 6 | 30px | Between related groups |
| 7 | 40px | Between sections of a custom panel |
| 8 | 50px | Between sections of a custom panel on a wall panel |
| 9 | 60px | Around the main heading of a custom panel |

<%= colour_example('layout-spacing-scale', callout: false) %>

This CSS gives a custom card 20px padding and 30px between 2 groups of controls.

```css
ha-card {
  padding: 20px;
}
.civica-group + .civica-group {
  margin-top: 30px;
}
```

### Native Home Assistant spacing

Home Assistant spaces its own cards with `--ha-space-1` to `--ha-space-20`, which go up in steps of 4px. Leave these as they are. civica does not override native padding to fit its 5px scale, because that would break the native layout.

## Surfaces and borders

Group content with spacing and headings first. Add a 1px border only where a group needs an edge. Do not put a box around a card, then around each section in it, then around each row.

| Edge | civica theme variable | Width |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Card edge or divider between rows | `civica-border-quiet` | 1px |
| Edge of a control people need to find | `civica-border-interactive` | 1px or 2px |
| Text field people type in | `civica-input-border` | 2px |
| Keyboard focus | `civica-focus-background` and `civica-focus-text` | 3px |

[Web-app colours](colour-web-app.html#functional-colours) gives the light and dark values for each variable. Do not weaken a text field's border or the focus style to make a busy dashboard look quieter.

Use square corners on maintained cards, buttons, fields and dialogs. Keep the native round shapes that carry meaning, such as a switch, a radio button, a [notification badge](../components/notification-badge.html), an avatar or a gauge. Do not add glows or large shadows.

## Dense dashboards

A home dashboard can hold more than a one-task form, because people use it every day. Keep labels readable and keep every control in maintained custom UI at least 44px high. Do not make text or controls smaller to fit more cards. Move less-used cards to another view instead.

Do not give a card with text a fixed height. A long room name or a translated label must be able to wrap.

## Dashboard background

Use a plain background behind controls and readings. A photograph or moving scene behind the whole dashboard makes text harder to read and draws attention from the cards. If a household wants a photograph, put it in a Picture card that has a job, such as showing the garden camera.

## Check the layout

Open the dashboard at each screen size in the table above, and at 200% text size. [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) gives the full reflow, zoom and target size checks. The whole page must not scroll sideways. A wide table or graph may scroll inside its own card.

Keep Home Assistant's own navigation. Do not hide the sidebar or the menu button to make a dashboard look tidier, because people need them to get back to their settings.
